AI Clinical Trial Card: Experimental Evidence on the Productivity Effects of Generative Artificial Intelligence

Authors: Shakked Noy, Whitney Zhang

AI Model Details

Provider: OpenAI
Model Name and Version: GPT-3.5 (via ChatGPT; exact backend version not specified)

Access Method:

Participants interacted with ChatGPT through the standard web interface

No custom integration or task-specific interface

Fine-tuning / Configuration:

No fine-tuning

Default ChatGPT system behavior; temperature and prompts not controlled

Human Participant Profile

Sample Size: 453

Population / Expertise:

College-educated professionals recruited online

Tasks designed to reflect general-purpose knowledge work, not domain-specific expert labor

AI Familiarity:

Study conducted shortly after ChatGPT’s release

Most participants likely had limited prior exposure

Interaction and Task Design

Task Description:

Writing tasks such as business memos, press releases, and short analytical texts

AI Role:

Assistive writing support

Experimental Conditions:

Control (no AI) vs. ChatGPT access

Outcomes:

Time to completion

Human-graded quality scores

Main Effects:

~40% reduction in completion time

~18% increase in quality

Larger gains for lower baseline performers

Interpretation Notes

Demonstrates strong productivity gains for generic writing tasks

Relies on a frictionless chat-based workflow rather than embedded tools

Results may differ for specialized or collaborative writing contexts